---
title: "A Category-Theory Track Measured by Its Checks, Not Its Name"
date: 2026-10-03
layout: post
---

{% include mathjax.html %}

*by <span class="icon-self">StrangeTcy</span>*

<dl class="epistemic-status">
<dt>Original ideas</dt>
<dd>Low to moderate. "Read the judge, not the folder" is an old methodological point; what is mine is the application to one archived track and the insistence on keeping its three specification gaps distinct.</dd>
<dt>Synthesis</dt>
<dd>Moderate to high. The post joins a frozen paid-run archive, a static placeholder scan of a clean source comparator, and a line-level source audit that were produced as separate artifacts.</dd>
<dt>Prose</dt>
<dd>Written through a multi-pass process: an internal source draft, two independent drafts, an editorial critique, and this final synthesis, all working from the same evidence dossier. No model runs were re-executed and nothing here is an independent replication.</dd>
<dt>Certainty</dt>
<dd>High for counts, exclusions, and what the inspected comparator files say. Medium for anything about the code that executed in paid workspaces, because the campaign recorded a dirty repository.</dd>
<dt>Importance</dt>
<dd>Modest and practical: a worked example of how much a benchmark name can promise that its checks do not deliver.</dd>
</dl>

Show me a benchmark track called `category_theoretic_compositional` and, before I have read a single task, I already believe something about it. Functors. Naturality. Morphisms that compose. The name has done my reading for me.

But the name never touched a submission. What produced each `PASS` and `FAIL` was a hidden judge and a handful of visible tests, and whatever those checked is what was measured. The name is a claim made by whoever assembled the tasks. The checks are the specification. If they disagree, the checks win, and the only way to find out is to read them.

This post does that for one track of one archived campaign. The conclusion is narrower than the track name, and I think the narrowing is the point.

## What I am looking at, and what I am not

The archive is a completed paid campaign of a model called Atria-Dawn-Preview, run against a specific source commit. Two facts about it govern everything below.

First, the campaign recorded its repository as **dirty**. All 33 selected configuration hashes match a clean source snapshot, and that snapshot is what I inspect. But a matching config hash is not byte-for-byte proof that every task prompt, visible test, judge, and helper file that ran in a paid workspace was identical to the clean one. So every source-level observation here is about a *comparator*, and I will keep saying so beside the claims it qualifies.

Second, each selected case was run once. One seed per cell, no environment-level replication. That rules out a whole category of inference before I start: five cells in an environment are five single draws, not a distribution, and certainly not a curve.

## Eighty-four rows, two kinds of pass

The track holds 85 recorded rows. One of them, a `sheaf_physical_constraints` case, carries a final note attributing its terminal failure to a provider transient after bounded retries. I set it aside as an explicit analysis exclusion; its raw score stays in the archive, I just do not read it as model performance. That leaves **84 eligible rows: 57 pass, 27 fail**, a rate near 0.68.

That number is the one I least want anyone to carry away, because it averages over two different scoring guarantees.

| Judge mode | Eligible | Pass | Fail | What the campaign says a pass certifies |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
| `behavioral_reference` | 5 | 4 | 1 | behaviour checked against a reference |
| `compile_only` | 79 | 53 | 26 | the submission built and cleared its checks; **exploratory** |

The campaign report itself calls compile-only results exploratory and excludes them from any validated aggregate. Seventy-nine of the 84 eligible rows are in that mode. The five behavioral-reference rows all belong to a single environment, `categorical_lenses`. So "57 of 84" is not a category-theory score with a confidence band around it. It is a heterogeneous code-repair artifact in which the validated part is five rows and the rest is, by the campaign's own label, a preliminary signal.

Across the track's 17 environments, per-environment outcomes run from zero eligible passes to perfect scores. The scalar hides that spread, and the spread is where the information is.

## A law stated, then not checked

`compositional_optimizer` is the cleanest case of the gap between prompt and judge.

In the clean comparator, the prompt describes strict associativity under nested composition, with numerical correctness carried across multiple training steps. That is a real categorical demand. Associativity says that for composable steps $f$, $g$, $h$,

$$(f \circ g) \circ h \;=\; f \circ (g \circ h),$$

and the version the prompt asks for is the strong one: the two parenthesisations should still agree after several optimizer updates, not only on the first.

Now the judge. In the comparator it checks output **shape** and a single state-isolation chain. The visible test is a single-step shape check. Both are perfectly reasonable programming checks. Neither can tell you whether two groupings of a three-operation composite stay equal over a multi-step horizon, because neither constructs two groupings or runs more than one step. The property is named in the prompt and asserted nowhere that grades the answer. A submission could violate the law in the environment's title and pass.

The environment's five outcomes—three passes, two failures, all compile-only—are therefore outcomes under shape and isolation checks, not evidence about associativity. And because the repository was dirty, even that is a statement about the inspected snapshot, not a guarantee about what ran.

## Knobs connected to nothing

The same environment shows a second problem, and this one is about the experimental design rather than the task.

Each configuration advertises two intervention axes, `naming` and `symptom_mask`, each at easy, medium, and hard. Laid out that way, five cells look like a small response surface. But a static scan of the clean templates finds that `compositional_optimizer`'s placeholders for both axes—`MODEL_CLASS` and `LR_VAL`—have no reference anywhere in the environment's task, starter file, visible test, or judge. Those files use a single momentum-step optimizer class directly, whatever the config says.

If a placeholder never appears in anything the agent sees or the judge checks, then turning it from easy to hard may change nothing downstream. The five cells collapse from a curve over optimizer classes and learning rates into five draws of roughly the same task. Reading the per-axis results confirms there is no structure to find: holding `symptom_mask` at easy and varying `naming` gives fail, pass, fail across easy, hard, medium; holding `naming` at easy and varying `symptom_mask` gives fail, pass, pass. With one seed per cell and possibly no live variable, that pattern is noise by construction.

The scan reports ten such no-reference control or variable axes across nine category environments. It is static—it does not execute task generation or diff every rendered bundle—so I treat the finding as *observed* about the source files and *inferred* about the executed runs. Nominal coverage can overstate the number of distinct interventions actually performed, and in this track it appears to.

Not every axis is dead. `sheaf_physical_constraints` has a `GLOBAL_CAP` placeholder that the templates do reference. That environment has a different problem, which I come to below.

## Laws checked, convention left to the visible test

`categorical_lenses` is the only environment here graded as `behavioral_reference`, and its four passes and one failure are the closest thing the track has to a validated result. It is also where the mismatch runs in the opposite direction, which I find clarifying.

The prompt describes a lens whose view is coordinate zero. The hidden judge checks the three lens laws—what the term conventionally means is the get–put, put–get, and put–put identities,

$$\mathrm{get}(\mathrm{put}(s,v)) = v,\qquad \mathrm{put}(s,\mathrm{get}(s)) = s,\qquad \mathrm{put}(\mathrm{put}(s,v),v') = \mathrm{put}(s,v'),$$

a real algebraic property. It does not itself assert the coordinate-zero convention the prompt emphasises; a visible test does. So the hidden judge guards the law suite, the visible test anchors the convention, and the prompt describes both. Each artifact carries a slice of the specification and none carries all of it. Meanwhile the config's `STRICT_LAWS` placeholder has no reference in the clean templates, and the judge checks all three laws regardless, so that axis too is a label without a lever.

The one failure is worth looking at for what kind of failure it is. Its verdict is `source_invalid`: a syntax error, an unindented block after a function definition. That is not a model that misunderstands lenses. That is a model that emitted code which would not parse. Four passes and one syntax error are exact outcomes under these checks; they are not a referendum on lens theory in either direction.

## Zero out of four, four different ways

`sheaf_physical_constraints` is the environment I would most expect to be misread, because its headline is a clean zero: no passes among four eligible rows. As a capability score that says the model cannot do sheaves. As checks-that-ran it says something smaller and more specific.

Start with what the judge asks for. The prompt requests capacity-safe routing. It does not state the 5:3 ratio that the inspected judge enforces on a fixed high-demand vector. The visible test checks output keys and shape, not that ratio. A submission can therefore fail by not inferring a numerical constraint it was never told—which is a reasonable thing to miss and not obviously a deficit in sheaf reasoning. The dirty-source caveat applies here as everywhere: this is what the comparator's judge does.

Then look at how the four eligible rows failed, because they did not fail alike.

| `naming` / `symptom_mask` | Score | Recorded failure | What the record shows |
|---|---:|---|---|
| easy / hard | 0.28 | `underfit` | ran, scored partial credit, missed the target |
| medium / easy | 0.28 | `underfit` | ran, scored partial credit, missed the target |
| easy / medium | 0.00 | `patch_invalid` | the patch file was empty |
| hard / easy | 0.00 | `invalid_action` | no valid JSON action could be parsed |

(The fifth recorded cell, easy / easy, is the provider-transient row excluded above.)

Only the two `underfit` rows are plausibly behavioural: something was submitted, it ran, and it scored partial credit against the hidden ratio. The other two never reached the question. One submitted nothing; one produced an action the harness could not parse. Folding a submission failure, a tooling failure, and two partial misses into "0/4" erases the one distinction a reader needs. Four events wearing the same `FAIL` are not four instances of the same event.

## The failure label is also a claim

That observation generalises, so I want to state it at the level where the numbers live. Across the campaign's full eligible set—192 cases after excluding two provider-terminal rows, a denominator distinct from this track's 84—the recorded failures split into 24 `patch_invalid` (every one an empty patch), 20 `underfit`, 10 `source_invalid`, 3 `runtime_error`, 2 `invalid_action`, and 2 `overfit_visible_tests`.

Those last two are the tell. Both carry a trusted score of 1.0 and notes about a missing required companion file. That is not what "overfit the visible tests" describes. The label and the recorded evidence disagree in at least those two rows, which is enough to settle how I read the `failure_mode` column everywhere: as a provisional campaign-and-judge tag, not a validated taxonomy of what went wrong inside the model.

Read that way, the track's own fails resolve into layers. An empty patch on one `compositional_optimizer` cell is a fact about the submission pipeline. A disallowed `weakref` import on another is a validator rule the model tripped. The syntax error in `categorical_lenses` is code that would not parse. None of these is a judgement about compositional reasoning, and all of them sit in the same scored column as the partial misses that might be. The behavioural signal in this track is thinner than 57 passes and 27 fails make it look, and it is thinner on both sides.

## What a contract would have to say

None of this is an argument that formal properties cannot be evaluated through code. Associativity, lens laws, and gluing constraints are all checkable; that is part of what makes them attractive as benchmark material. It is an argument that the evaluation needs a contract connecting five things that in this track drift apart: the mathematical claim, the prompt, the visible tests, the hidden judge, and the reference implementation.

Concretely, for each task I would want the authors to state the property under test in one sentence; to supply counterexamples and valid alternative implementations; and to have an independent oracle confirm that the hidden tests separate the intended property from plausible code that merely compiles. For the axes, the fix is a build step rather than better prose: every advertised intervention must demonstrably change at least one agent-visible or judge-visible artifact, or the configuration fails to build. An axis that moves nothing should not be allowed to inflate a coverage count silently.

There is precedent for wanting the contract explicit rather than averaged away. HELM's multi-scenario, multi-metric design and VarBench's dynamic variable perturbation—with several seeds per variable-based experiment—both exist because a single scalar over heterogeneous tasks hides too much. I cite them as the shape of the worry, not as validation of this campaign, and this targeted check supports no claim of novelty.

## What this evidence licenses

Here is the narrowest sentence the archive supports. On these selected code-repair tasks, under these particular judges and visible tests, almost entirely in an exploratory compile-only mode, with one seed per cell and a dirty repository behind the inspected comparator, Atria-Dawn-Preview produced a mixed set of submissions: 57 of 84 eligible rows passed, per-environment outcomes ran from zero to perfect, and a meaningful share of the failures were empty patches, syntax errors, disallowed imports, and unparseable actions rather than conceptual misses.

Here is what it does not license. It does not show that the model understands category theory, or sheaf consistency, or compositionality as general concepts, because in three of the inspected environments the thing being scored and the thing being named are not the same thing. It does not supply a single validated category-theory score, because the track mixes two judge guarantees and no common calibration across its environments has been demonstrated. It does not place `hard` and `easy` cells on a difficulty axis, because for several axes there is no evidence the lever was connected to anything. And it does not show that the model *cannot* do these things either; a shape check that cannot detect an associativity violation also cannot detect associativity.

What the archive can do is point at exactly which prompts, axes, and judges to repair first. That is a useful thing for a benchmark run to produce. It is just not the thing the folder name promised, and the moment I let that name do my reading, I measured the folder instead of the model.
